diff --git a/docs/inventory-screen.md b/docs/inventory-screen.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3712799
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/inventory-screen.md
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# Inventory screen
+
+The inventory screen shows all ten block types, how many of each the player has, which ones are
+locked and at what level they unlock, and lets the player pick the block to build with. Most
+players are kids on phones, so touch comes first: big targets, no hover-only information, and
+as few steps as possible. Mouse and keyboard, and controllers, get the same screen with their own
+shortcuts.
+
+Code: `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/` (`InventoryApp.luau` holds the state and
+controls; `Components/` holds the pieces). `Main.client.luau` starts it.
+
+## Layout
+
+- **Blocks button** (always on screen when the inventory is closed): bottom centre, 84 px square,
+  between the phone thumbstick and the jump button. It shows the block being built with and how
+  many are left, so it also tells the player what they're holding.
+- **Panel**: centred over a dimmed backdrop, up to 760 x 560 px, inside the device safe area.
+  - Header: "My Blocks", the player's level, and a 56 px close button.
+  - Grid: one tile per block type, in hotbar order. Up to five columns; the tile size is worked
+    out from the space available so all ten fit without scrolling on a landscape phone. On very
+    small screens the grid scrolls rather than shrinking tiles below about 88 px.
+  - Footer: the controls for the current device, or a short message after a failed pick.
+- **Tile**: block picture, name, and either the count (`x48`, `None yet` when it's 0) or, when
+  locked, a lock over the picture and `Level 4` in gold. Everything is on the tile itself, so
+  nothing needs hover. The block being built with has a green outline and a tick; the tile under
+  the mouse or controller cursor gets a white outline and grows slightly.
+
+## Opening and closing
+
+| | Touch | Mouse and keyboard | Controller |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Open | Tap **Blocks** | Click **Blocks** or press **B** | Press **Y** |
+| Close | Tap **X**, or tap outside the panel | Click **X**, click outside the panel, or press **B** | Press **B** or **Y** |
+| Closes by itself | After picking a block | After picking a block | After picking a block |
+
+While the screen is open, placing blocks in the world is switched off so a tap or click that
+misses the panel never builds anything, and the mouse is freed even in first person or shift
+lock. Opening it again restores whatever placement state it was in before.
+
+## Picking a block
+
+| | Touch | Mouse and keyboard | Controller |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Move between blocks | (not needed) | Move the mouse | D-pad or left stick; the cursor starts on the current block and can't leave the grid |
+| Pick | Tap the tile | Click the tile, or press **1**-**9**, **0** (works with the screen closed too) | Press **A** |
+| Locked block | Tap shows "Ice unlocks at level 4. Keep building!" | Same, on click | Same, on **A** |
+| Block with none | Tap shows "You don't have any Window yet. Go collect some!" | Same, on click | Same, on **A** |
+
+Picking a block the player can use selects it with `BlockPlacer.setBlockType` and closes the
+screen: one tap from "open" to "building again". Locked and empty blocks stay on the screen so
+players can see what's coming, but picking one only shows the message in the footer for three
+seconds. The number keys follow the same rule (`BlockPlacer.canBuildWith`), and their keycaps are
+drawn only on tiles they can pick.
+
+## Hints per device
+
+The UI follows the device the player last used (`UserInputService.LastInputTypeChanged`) and
+swaps hints on the fly:
+
+- **Touch**: no button hints, just "Tap a block to build with it".
+- **Mouse and keyboard**: keycaps for **B** on the Blocks and close buttons, the number key in each
+  tile's corner, and "1-0 Pick, B Close" in the footer.
+- **Controller**: the platform's own button images (Xbox or PlayStation) for **Y** on the Blocks
+  button, **B** on close, and "A Pick, B Close" in the footer.
+
+Picking up a controller while the screen is open puts the cursor on the current block; touching
+the screen or moving the mouse hides the cursor again.
+
+## Not covered yet
+
+- The server doesn't check unlock levels or counts when placing, and placing doesn't use up
+  blocks. The screen and number keys only stop the player from picking those blocks on the client.
+- Escape still opens the Roblox menu; it doesn't close the inventory.
